#!/bin/bash

# omarchy:summary=Run pending Omarchy migrations

# Omarchy ships migration scripts (218 at v4.5.0) that bring an *older* Arch
# install forward. Many use sudo, write under /usr or call pacman, so on NixOS
# they range from meaningless to destructive: there is no /usr to write to, no
# pacman to call, and the things they migrate -- release channels, keyrings,
# system config -- are the flake's job here.
#
# With no state directory every one of them counts as pending, so this is not
# academic. Someone running `omarchy migrate` on a machine installed today
# would have run all of them as root.
#
# The ones that edit a user's own files are decided one by one in
# data/user-migrations.nix; those that apply here are ported into
# omarchy-migrate-user, which a post-boot hook runs (#1610).
#
# --pending answers honestly rather than refusing: nothing IS pending, because
# nothing here will ever run them. omarchy-bar and omarchy-migrate-notify both
# ask that way, and an error where they expect a list would be a worse answer
# than the true one. A bare run is a person, and gets told why.
set -euo pipefail

case "${1-}" in
  --pending | --check)
    # No output, exit 0: the contract for "nothing to do".
    exit 0
    ;;
  -h | --help)
    echo "Usage: omarchy migrate [--pending]"
    exit 0
    ;;
esac

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
Migrations are not how a NixOS install moves forward.

  Omarchy's migrations bring an older Arch system up to date -- they run
  pacman, write under /usr, and edit system config with sudo. None of that
  is how this machine is built.

  The equivalent here is your flake:

    nix flake update nixarchy      pick up a newer Omarchy
    omarchy update                 the same thing, from the menu

  Nixarchy pins one Omarchy release, and moving between releases happens by
  changing that pin and rebuilding -- with the packaging updated to match,
  which is work migrations cannot do from inside a running system.
MSG
exit 1
